; ========================================================================
; macro_and_data.asm - documented ACME source
; Every code-bearing line has WHAT, WHY and HOW comments.
; WHAT describes the operation. WHY explains its role. HOW explains encoding,
; flags, addressing, hardware effects or control flow.
; Code comments do not change the generated program bytes.
; PURPOSE: Demonstrate ACME macros, aligned data, generated bytes and an assembly-time
; assertion.
; ASSUMPTIONS: Assembled at $2000 without a BASIC launcher. Load to its header address and SYS
; 8192 to call its short routine.
; ASSEMBLE: Select CBM format and an output name using the command line.
; Example from the companion root:
; acme --strict-segments -f cbm -o demo.prg reference_examples/macro_and_data.asm
; Archived originals are unchanged and are not the recommended teaching copies.
; ========================================================================
; WHAT: Select the NMOS 6510 instruction set.
; WHY: Generate instructions for the C64 CPU rather than a later 65C02.
; HOW: In ACME 0.97, 6510 aliases nmos6502 and also permits undocumented opcodes; this course uses official instructions.
!cpu 6510
; WHAT: Place the following output at $2000.
; WHY: Keep the BASIC launcher, executable code or graphics at its planned address.
; HOW: This is assembly-time placement, not a runtime jump. CBM output fills gaps between the lowest and highest emitted addresses.
* = $2000
; WHAT: Define macro put with value and address parameters.
; WHY: Reuse a two-instruction store pattern at assembly time.
; HOW: ACME records the block for later expansion; defining it does not emit its instructions yet.
!macro put .value, .address {
; WHAT: Load A from .value (literal value).
; WHY: Set the literal value needed to perform this step.
; HOW: Immediate operand: the value is encoded in the instruction. Flags updated: N Z. 
    lda #.value
; WHAT: Copy A to .address.
; WHY: Update .address while we process this section.
; HOW: Read or write the named memory address; ACME chooses its supported encoding. Status flags are unchanged. 
    sta .address
; WHAT: End the macro definition.
; WHY: Delimit the instructions that each +put invocation will expand.
; HOW: This closing brace is assembler syntax and emits no CPU opcode.
}
; WHAT: Expand put with value 5 and address $D020.
; WHY: Generate a green-border assignment from the reusable macro.
; HOW: The expansion emits LDA #5 followed by STA $D020; it is not a runtime JSR.
+put 5, $d020
; WHAT: Return from the current subroutine.
; WHY: Return control to the caller after this routine has completed its work.
; HOW: Pull the JSR return address, add one, and continue there. The stack must still match the call; flags are unchanged.
rts
; WHAT: Pad output to the next 64-byte boundary.
; WHY: Ensure the sprite slot can be represented by a hardware sprite pointer.
; HOW: ACME emits zero bytes until (* AND 63)=0; no padding is needed if already aligned.
!align 63,0,0
; WHAT: Define address label sprite.
; WHY: Name the entry or data location used to organise this section.
; HOW: ACME records the current assembly address; a label alone emits no bytes.
sprite:
; WHAT: Emit the stated count of repeated bytes.
; WHY: Allocate and initialise sprite or the sprite artwork.
; HOW: ACME creates data during assembly; !fill 12,0 produces twelve zeros, while !fill 63,$ff produces 21 solid three-byte sprite rows.
!fill 64,0
; WHAT: Generate values 0 through 15.
; WHY: Build a small table without typing every byte.
; HOW: The inclusive assembly-time loop executes !byte i sixteen times and emits sixteen bytes.
!for i,0,15 { !byte i }
; WHAT: Check the sprite address alignment at build time.
; WHY: Fail instead of producing a misaligned graphics layout.
; HOW: If sprite AND 63 is nonzero, !error rejects the build; no runtime test is emitted.
!if sprite AND 63 { !error "sprite misaligned" }
